  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Weequery 3.0.0. A large release; the README covers all of it.\n\nBREAKING. Inquiry is immutable. ApplyCondition, ApplySorts and every Bind call return a new Inquiry rather than\nchanging the one they were called on, so a configured Inquiry can be kept and branched and neither branch can\nreach the other. A chain that discards the return value no longer accumulates anything.\n\nBREAKING. QueryStyle.Native is the default for reading and writing query strings. QueryStyle.CSharp and\nQueryStyle.Sql are deprecated and will be removed in 4.0; pass one explicitly to keep the old spelling.\n\nAdded: binding resolution, so BindResolve walks the entity and binds what it finds, with\nBindingResolutionSettings to subtract before it does. Collections, with BindCollection and Any, All and None\nover their elements. Projection, so a caller chooses the columns as well as the rows, with * and Prefix.*\nwildcards. Paging, through BuildPaged and PagedQuery. BindingUse, so filtering, sorting and projection are\ngranted separately and RemoveBinding revokes any of them, by name or by wildcard. ListBindings, which reads the\nallow-list back so you can see what resolution opened up. Validate, which reports what a query would do before\nit runs. InquirySettings.IgnoreUnboundFields, which drops what it cannot bind rather than refusing the query and\nreports it in DroppedFields. QueryRequest, carrying filter, sort, projection and paging in one object. A\nWeequeryError code on every refusal. Value conversion, per-backend operator support, and trimming and Native AOT\nannotations throughout.\n\nCompanion packages for EF Core, OData, Elasticsearch, AutoMapper, Mapster and Mapperly are released alongside at\nthe same version.",
